docs {
  The full ticket lifecycle: submit, assign, reassign, request info,
  provide info, add a processing note, complete, and close. Every request
  here uses the admin token from auth/login: system_admin holds
  ticket:create (as every role does), ticket:manage and ticket:read_all,
  and — as this folder's own requester — the requester-only actions
  (provide-info, close-after-completion) too. That is not a shortcut around
  authorization; it means every request in this folder is a real,
  independently-authorized call, just one where the same account happens to
  qualify under more than one of the rules — see internal/ticket's Service
  methods for what each rule actually is.

  resolve-admin-id runs first to capture the admin's own user id (nothing
  in /auth/login returns it), since assign needs a real assignee_id and
  tickets.assignee_id is a foreign key into users.

  Tickets have no delete endpoint — closed is the terminal state, not
  removal — so this folder does not "leave nothing behind" the way users/
  and org/ do. That is by design: an audit trail for a closed ticket is
  meant to persist.
}
